  <div class="callout callout--tip">
    <span class="callout__heading">✦ Tip for new arrivals</span>
    <p>
      If you're arriving without a fixed address, consider booking a short-term let or Airbnb
      for your first 2–4 weeks. It gives you time to view flats in person rather than rushing
      into a decision from abroad. Viewing in person is strongly advised — photos can be very
      misleading, especially for location.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- ── What it costs ──────────────────── -->
  <h2>What to budget for</h2>

  <p>
    Before searching, it helps to understand the full upfront cost — not just the monthly rent.
  </p>

  <h3>The deposit</h3>
  <p>
    Landlords can charge a maximum of <strong>5 weeks' rent</strong> as a deposit (for
    properties with annual rent under £50,000). This is capped by law under the
    <strong>Tenant Fees Act 2019</strong>. So if your rent is £1,600/month, your deposit
    cannot exceed £1,846 (£1,600 × 12 ÷ 52 × 5).
  </p>

  <h3>Holding deposit</h3>
  <p>
    When you agree to take a property, you may be asked for a holding deposit of up to
    <strong>1 week's rent</strong> to reserve it while referencing is completed. This should
    be deducted from your first month's rent or returned — not charged on top.
  </p>

  <h3>First month's rent</h3>
  <p>
    Usually paid upfront when you sign. Some landlords ask for the first and last month,
    though last month in advance is less common since the deposit cap came in.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout--note">
    <span class="callout__heading">Letting agent fees are banned</span>
    <p>
      Since the Tenant Fees Act 2019, letting agents <strong>cannot charge tenants</strong>
      for referencing, administration, viewings or tenancy setup. If an agent tries to charge
      you any of these fees, they are breaking the law. Report it to your local council's
      trading standards team.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- ── Referencing ─────────────────────── -->
  <h2>The referencing process</h2>

  <p>
    Once you've had an offer accepted, the landlord or agent will run reference checks.
    This typically involves:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Credit check</strong> — a soft or hard search of your UK credit history. If you've just arrived, your UK credit history will be thin. This is common and most landlords know it — having a good international credit history, bank statements, or a guarantor helps.</li>
    <li><strong>Income/employment check</strong> — most landlords require your annual salary to be at least <strong>30× the monthly rent</strong>. So for a £1,500/month flat, you'd need to earn at least £45,000/year. If you're self-employed, you'll usually need 2 years of accounts or SA302 tax returns.</li>
    <li><strong>Previous landlord reference</strong> — a reference confirming you paid rent on time and left the property in good condition.</li>
    <li><strong>Right to Rent check</strong> — landlords are legally required to check you have the right to rent in the UK. You'll need to show your passport and visa/BRP.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout callout--tip">
    <span class="callout__heading">✦ If you fail referencing</span>
    <p>
      Don't panic — it's common for new arrivals. Options include: offering a
      <strong>guarantor</strong> (someone, usually UK-based, who agrees to cover rent if you
      can't), paying several months' rent upfront (landlord's choice to accept), or finding a
      landlord who lets through OpenRent and is more flexible. Explain your situation honestly
      — many landlords understand the catch-22 of being new to the country.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- ── Tenancy agreement ──────────────── -->
  <h2>Understanding your tenancy agreement</h2>

  <p>
    Most private rentals in England are on an <strong>Assured Shorthold Tenancy (AST)</strong>.
    Read the whole thing before signing — it's a legal contract. Key things to look for:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Tenancy length</strong> — usually 6 or 12 months fixed term, after which it may roll into a monthly periodic tenancy</li>
    <li><strong>Rent review clause</strong> — check whether and how the landlord can increase your rent</li>
    <li><strong>Break clause</strong> — does either party have the right to end the tenancy early? If so, how much notice is required?</li>
    <li><strong>What's included</strong> — is it furnished or unfurnished? Are white goods included?</li>
    <li><strong>Permitted occupants</strong> — who is allowed to live there. Sub-letting restrictions.</li>
    <li><strong>Pet policy</strong> — explicitly stated, not assumed</li>
    <li><strong>Repairs and maintenance</strong> — the landlord is responsible for the structure, heating, hot water and most appliances they provide. You are responsible for minor repairs and keeping it clean.</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- ── Deposit protection ─────────────── -->
  <h2>Deposit protection — your money is protected by law</h2>

  <p>
    Your landlord is legally required to place your deposit in one of three
    <strong>government-approved Tenancy Deposit Schemes</strong> within 30 days of receiving
    it, and to give you written confirmation (called "prescribed information") of which scheme
    holds it. Keep this document.
  </p>

  <p>The three schemes are:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>TDS</strong> — Tenancy Deposit Scheme (<a href="https://www.tenancydepositscheme.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">tenancydepositscheme.com</a>)</li>
    <li><strong>DPS</strong> — Deposit Protection Service (<a href="https://www.depositprotection.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">depositprotection.com</a>)</li>
    <li><strong>MyDeposits</strong> — (<a href="https://www.mydeposits.co.uk" target="_blank" rel="noopener">mydeposits.co.uk</a>)</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    At the end of your tenancy, the landlord has 10 days to return your deposit (or the agreed
    portion of it) after you've both agreed any deductions. If there's a dispute, the scheme
    provides a free adjudication service — you don't need a solicitor.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout--note">
    <span class="callout__heading">Protect yourself</span>
    <p>
      Always complete a thorough <strong>check-in inventory</strong> on the day you move in —
      with photos and written notes of any existing damage. Sign only if it's accurate. This is
      your main protection against unfair deposit deductions when you leave.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- ── Tenant rights ────────────────────── -->
  <h2>Your rights as a tenant</h2>

  <p>
    UK tenant protections are reasonably strong. Key rights include:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Right to a safe home</strong> — the property must be free from hazards, have working heating and hot water, and be free from damp and mould. If it isn't, report it in writing and keep a record.</li>
    <li><strong>Right to quiet enjoyment</strong> — your landlord must give at least 24 hours' notice before entering (except in genuine emergencies).</li>
    <li><strong>Protection from illegal eviction</strong> — a landlord cannot evict you without a court order, cannot change the locks, and cannot harass you into leaving. This is a criminal offence.</li>
    <li><strong>Deposit protection</strong> — as above. If your landlord fails to protect your deposit in a scheme, you can claim up to 3× the deposit amount in court.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout callout--official">
    <span class="callout__heading">Renters Reform — the law is changing</span>
    <p>
      The UK government has been legislating significant changes to tenant rights, including
      plans to abolish no-fault (Section 21) evictions. The legal position may have changed
      since this guide was written — check the current status on gov.uk.
    </p>
    <ul class="official-links">
      <li>
        <a href="https://www.gov.uk/private-renting" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
          Private renting rights — gov.uk
        </a>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <!-- ── Before you sign ──────────────────── -->
  <h2>Checklist: before you sign</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>✓ Check the deposit will be registered in a government-approved scheme</li>
    <li>✓ Confirm the landlord has a gas safety certificate (renewed annually — ask to see it)</li>
    <li>✓ Ask for the Energy Performance Certificate (EPC) — properties must be rated E or above to be legally let</li>
    <li>✓ Check if it's an HMO (House in Multiple Occupation) — if 3+ unrelated people share, the property needs an HMO licence</li>
    <li>✓ Take photos of every room, every appliance and any damage before you move anything in</li>
    <li>✓ Read the tenancy agreement in full — check the break clause, notice period and rent review clause</li>
    <li>✓ Confirm who pays council tax (usually the tenant)</li>
    <li>✓ Get contents insurance — the landlord's insurance does not cover your belongings</li>
  </ul>

